            <h2 class="section-title">SECTION 1</h2>
            <h3>DISCOVERING BOSTON AND YOUR PLACE IN THE CITY</h3>
            <div class="two-column-layout">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Mission</h3>
                    <p>The Off-Campus Housing Office (OCHO) is a resource for all current or future students, staff, and faculty looking for off-campus housing in the Boston area. OCHO provides support and assistance to enhance the housing search experience for all members of the Suffolk community. We offer programs and services that advocate for your rights as a renter and help you navigate the complexities of the Boston rental market.</p>
                    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Overview of Boston</h3>
                    <p>Welcome to your first year of living off campus in Boston! While it is the largest city in New England, Boston is essentially a collection of neighborhoods. As you will discover, each neighborhood has a personality all its own. It’s your job to choose the neighborhood that best suits your personality, needs, and financial means. This handbook aims to empower you with the information you will need in order to feel at home in this historic, vibrant city.</p>
                    <p>Boston is home to more than 90 universities and colleges, and a remarkably high concentration of students and young professionals. These demographics result in a highly competitive rental market. With careful planning, determination, and advice from the OCHO, you can conduct a successful housing hunt.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Overview of the Housing Search with OCHO</h3>
                    <p>OCHO provides resources for every stage of the housing search, from finding a neighborhood to finding a roommate or landlord contacts. Every year, we host events to help students learn about the rental market, meet local resources, and learn how to find a place that fits their needs. Please be sure to visit <span data-field="ocho_website_url">{{ ocho_website_url }}</span> for the most current information.</p>
                    <p>The process starts with a visit to our office. We can help you understand the rental market and help you with every step of your housing search. First, you should decide who you will live with. Next, you will start looking for a neighborhood. Once you have a neighborhood in mind, you can start looking for apartments. Once you have found a place, we can help you with the lease and other important documents. We also provide resources to help you with the move-in process. If you are looking for housing for the fall semester, we recommend starting your search as early as possible. If you are looking for housing for the spring semester, begin your search in the fall. We also offer workshops on topics such as renter's rights, how to budget, and how to find a roommate. Don't forget to check our website for more information.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="content-body">
            <h2 class="section-title">SECTION 2</h2>
            <h3>HUNTING FOR YOUR NEW HOME</h3>
            <p>The Off Campus Housing Office (OCHO) provides information to Suffolk University students as a courtesy service. Information is for the use of Suffolk University students only and is updated periodically. Suffolk University neither accepts nor creates any responsibility for the reliability of the information provided or those listing with the OCHO. We do not endorse any listings, nor are we involved in any rental agreements. Suffolk University is not a real estate agent, broker, or other agency. All listings are provided on an as-is basis, and any agreement is between you and the property owner or landlord.</p>
            <p>Persons interested in the listed opportunities should investigate those opportunities on their own. We strongly advise you to investigate those opportunities before making any commitments. By using this welcome kit and OCHO's additional resources, you agree to assume all risks associated with your housing search. Suffolk University and its employees and other agents from any liability and responsibility for any claims for any personal injury, accident, damage, expense, or other loss that may be incurred by you, or to you or your property.</p>

            <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Off-Campus Partners</h3>
            <p>Your search for roommates, neighborhoods, and even furniture should begin on our website. <span data-field="ocho_website_url">{{ ocho_website_url }}</span>. Once you are on our website, you can find a list of off-campus partners who are real estate agents and other universities look for potential roommates. These partners are vetted by OCHO, but we cannot guarantee that they are the best fit for you. We also have a list of resources to help you with your housing search.</p>

            <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Attending OCHO Events</h3>
            <p>The Off-Campus Housing Office organizes special events designed to kick-start your housing search. These events are designed to help you learn about the rental market, meet local resources, and connect with other students who are also looking for housing. All Transitions events are informative and fun!</p>
        </div>
    </div>

